BE-300 2.6.8.1: keep sys_pipe()'s second descriptor store.

Same hazard as 2.4 patch 0014: the write end goes back in $v1 of the
trap frame that the by-value `regs' argument aliases, a store GCC 10 can
delete as dead.  2.6.8.1 already declares the parameter volatile; make
the store go through an explicit volatile pointer too, matching 2.4.

---
 arch/mips/kernel/syscall.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/mips/kernel/syscall.c b/arch/mips/kernel/syscall.c
index 16519f7e8c3bafd608fe573d7407a8dc82fa1748..77e96c60b84884aa5baa845e137a9f6ab373e14c 100644
--- a/arch/mips/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/arch/mips/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -46,7 +46,13 @@ asmlinkage int sys_pipe(nabi_no_regargs volatile struct pt_regs regs)
 		res = error;
 		goto out;
 	}
-	regs.regs[3] = fd[1];
+	/*
+	 * The write end goes back in $v1 of the caller's trap frame, which
+	 * `regs' (passed by value on the stack) aliases.  GCC 10 sees a dead
+	 * store to a by-value argument and deletes it, so every pipe() got a
+	 * garbage second descriptor; store through a volatile pointer.
+	 */
+	((volatile struct pt_regs *)&regs)->regs[3] = fd[1];
 	res = fd[0];
 out:
 	return res;
